data.content:
## What happened

In C# 14 / .NET 10, overload resolution changed for extension methods on array types. When you write `array.Contains(num)` on an `int[]`, the compiler now resolves to:

```
MemoryExtensions.Contains<T>(ReadOnlySpan<T>, T)
```

instead of the previous:

```
Enumerable.Contains<T>(IEnumerable<T>, T)
```

Because `T[]` implicitly converts to `ReadOnlySpan<T>`, and C# 14's improved extension resolution considers this a *better* match. Since `ReadOnlySpan<T>` is a **ref struct**, it cannot be represented in expression trees. The expression compiles, but the interpreter blows up at runtime when it tries to invoke the span-based method.

## How to fix it

Disambiguate by calling `Enumerable.Contains` explicitly so the compiler doesn't pick the span overload:

```csharp
using System.Linq;

// Option 1: Static method syntax (recommended for expression trees)
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => Enumerable.Contains(array, num);

// Option 2: Build the call node manually
var arrayParam = Expression.Parameter(typeof(int[]), "array");
var numParam   = Expression.Parameter(typeof(int), "num");

var containsMethod = typeof(Enumerable)
    .GetMethod(nameof(Enumerable.Contains))!
    .MakeGenericMethod(typeof(int));

var call = Expression.Call(containsMethod, arrayParam, numParam);
var expr = Expression.Lambda<Func<int[], int, bool>>(call, arrayParam, numParam);
```

Both approaches pin the resolution to `Enumerable.Contains`, which operates on `IEnumerable<T>` and is fully supported by the expression tree interpreter.
